branchT -- SEQ 476 (Tony, 2026-10-30): break, continue and blocks on the new road, and two crash fixes. PRE-REGISTERED ON
TRUNK before the build.

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds two inputs per row, one process each under an alarm: everything above this file's
`stop();`, then the row on the OLD road (the statement itself) or on the NEW road (the rule called by name, then testOP on
what it handed back, then the optional 4th field, OLD-road statements run after the call), then `BT NEXT` and a sentinel.

R4, MEASURED (2026-10-30): the old road never refuses a stray break or continue. A block `{ }` stops at the break and so
does an action body; the next statement runs (1 3). Bare top-level statements ignore it (`print 1:; break; print 2:;`
prints 1 2). STARt is a seq, and macros says an action body is a seq, so the intended value is the block's: STARt stops at the
break, nothing refuses, and the next top-level statement runs (BR-2). A second STARt is NOT used as the later seq: today a
second STARt built in one process carries the first one's instruct entries (seq stmts seq stmts) and opSeq is handed `seq`
as its list -- pre-existing, outside this stroke (TODO). opSeq clears branchKind before each statement, as aCTionBlocK
does, so a stale signal cannot cut a later seq short.

TG-1: a raw label whose first child carries a method (testOP, set by name on tgA before it is attached) -- testOP must refuse it
by name, not call it as an operator. The method is testOP because the setting statement's value is RUN at the top level, and
testOP on a childless node hands it back; called as an OPERATOR with null operands it dereferences null. (The pre-registered
patient set processAction, and that statement itself fired processAction -- its exit 139 was the patient, not testOP: corrected
SEQ 476 before the build was certified.)

TODAY: BR-1 prints 1 2 (BRANCh builds nothing, STARt skips it); BL-1 prints nothing (BLOCk builds nothing); IF-5 and IF-6
die with exit 139 in the parse (If attaches a null arm); TG-1 dies with exit 139 in processAction.

IF-7 (added SEQ 476, H17): with BRANCh and BLOCk building, IF-5 and IF-6 no longer reach R6's refusal, so its control could
not go red on them. An arm that hands up nothing today is a refused return; If must refuse by name and the next statement run.

SL-1, SL-2 (SEQ 478 R0, the STARt leak): two STARts built in one process each run right; two sibling blocks in one STARt
each run right. Today the second of each reads `nextGroup: ERROR seq does not contain a list`: its instruct still holds the
first one's pieces (measured: STARt's `instruct` is length 2 at its second entry; `stmts` is 0).

